From 62843095c6a598ae84accd6991c13ae018e36196 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:04:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] hsdir: Refuse HS dir requests whose previous hop is not a
 known relay

Since 0.4.2.1-alpha (24964) HSDirs refuse onion service descriptor
fetches and uploads from client channels, so they can't be made over
one-hop circuits. But a peer that authenticates with any self-generated
relay identity is not a client channel, so it can still make one-hop HS
requests, which also escape DoSCircuitCreation at the HSDir.

As exits have done since 0.2.2 (1751), check whether the previous hop is
a known relay (in our consensus, or with a descriptor). This rejects
arbitrary unknown prev-hop identities; it does not rate limit anything,
since an attacker can still add one hop through any consensus relay. It
closes the one-hop escape that the previous commit would otherwise push
attackers into.

This is a separate predicate, connection_dir_prev_hop_is_known_relay(),
reached through connection_dir_check_prev_hop() at the two HS call sites
with its own 503 reason -- not a second condition inside
connection_dir_is_anonymous(), whose client-channel check (and the exit
path's RefuseUnknownExits) are left untouched. Counting always happens;
refusing is off by default and is its own HSDirRefuseUnknownPrevHop
torrc option and hsdir_refuse_unknown_prev_hop consensus parameter,
independent of RefuseUnknownExits, so an Exit+HSDir operator can roll
back the HSDir behavior alone.

Stock C tor, Arti and Onionbalance always have a consensus relay as the
previous hop. The only false positives are in the roughly 1e-6
per-request window when the relay has not yet seen the previous hop in a
fresh consensus.

Part of 41339.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
